 to remember that the derivative represents a rate of change.\n\nFor instance, suppose that the temperature of a pizza $t$ minutes after coming out of a hot oven is $T(t)$ degrees Fahrenheit. What are the units of $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t}?$\n\nFirst, we have that the temperature $T(t)$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit and the time $t$ is measured in minutes.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t): & \\text{degrees Fahrenheit} \\\\ t: & \\text{minutes}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}T}{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}t}$ measures a change in ${\\color{black}T}$ over a change in ${\\color{black}t}.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}: & \\frac{\\text{degrees Fahrenheit}}{\\text{minutes}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}T}{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}t}$ is measured in \"degrees Fahrenheit per minute\", or $^\\circ \\textrm F / \\textrm {min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065394 #edb/ref 1065 552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065394 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1a148fb4-165b-500a-b7e6-a3c9c2776ae8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065394 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327037 #edb/ref 1080 "The distance traveled increased by $10$ meters during the first $30$ seconds" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327037 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327037 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0be07498-8050-537a-b25e-fd95bab4699a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327231 #edb/ref 1080 "$10$ hours after launching, the number of visitors decreased at a rate of $1\\,500$ visitors per hour" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327231 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327231 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "224a9606-2cff-583a-a5a5-2ad7bd61c0d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327275 #edb/ref 1080 "$10$ hours after launching, the number of visitors increased by a total of $1\\,500$ 