\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}: & \\frac{\\text{squared centimeters}}{\\text{packages}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}$ is measured in \"squared centimeters per package\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517216 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517226 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517216 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517216 #edb/ref 1071 "A farmer estimates that she can sell $w(p)$ metric kilograms of crops at a price of $p$ dollars per kilogram. What are the units of $\\frac{dw}{dp}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517216 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b76a707c-559e-596a-94b2-80c91183a360" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517216 #edb/ref 1074 "We have that $w(p)$ is measured in kilograms and $p$ is measured in dollars per kilogram. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w(p): & \\text{kilograms} \\\\ p: & \\text{dollars per kilogram}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}w}{\\text{d}p}$ measures a change in $w(p)$ over a change in $p.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}w}{\\text{d}p}: & \\frac{\\text{kilograms}}{\\text{dollars per kilogram}}=\\frac{\\text{squared kilograms}}{\\text{dollars}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\text{d}w}{\\text{d}p}$ is measured in \"squared kilograms per dollar\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517224 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517215 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517224 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517224 #edb/ref 1071 "A farmer estimates that she can sell $w(p)$ metric kilograms of crops at a price of $p$ euros per kilogram. What are the units of $\\frac{dw}{dp}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517224 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c8fff33f-dcae-5a21-b571-f887d084165f" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517224 #edb/ref 1074 "We have that $w(p)$ is measured in kilograms and $p$ is measured in euros per kilogram. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w(p): & \\text{kilograms} \\\\ p: & \\text{euros per kilogram}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}w}{\\text{d}p}$ measures a change in $w(p)$ over a change in $p.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}w}{\\text{d}p}: & \\frac{\\text{kilograms}}{\\text{euros per kilogram}}=\\frac{\\text{squared kilograms}}{\\text{euros}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\text{d}w}{\\text{d}p}$ is measured in \"squared kilograms per euro\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517229 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517201 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517229 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517229 #edb/ref 1071 "A supplier sells $Q(x)$ items of a product at a price of $x$ euros per item. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517229 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d9bf87bc-d76d-5ddd-bb05-61b9ce4126ef" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517229 #edb/ref 1074 "We have that the quantity $Q(x)$ is measured in items and the input $x$ is measured in euros per item. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x): & \\text{items} \\\\ x: & \\text{euros per item}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}$ measures a change in $Q(x)$ over a change in $x.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}: & \\frac{\\text{items}}{\\text{euros per item}}=\\frac{\\text{squared items}}{\\text{euros}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}$ is measured in \"squared items per euro\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517230 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517181 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517230 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517230 #edb/ref 1071 "A supplier sells $Q(x)$ meters of a product at a price of $x$ dollars per meter. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517230 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "de6c4a4c-15b1-5b19-99a8-789f150daca7" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517230 #edb/ref 1074 "We have that the quantity $Q(x)$ is measured in meters and the input $x$ is measured in dollars per meter. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x): & \\text{meters} \\\\ x: & \\text{dollars per meter}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}$ measures a change in $Q(x)$ over a change in $x.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}: & \\frac{\\text{meters}}{\\text{dollars per meter}}=\\frac{\\text{squared meters}}{\\text{dollars}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}$ is measured in \"squared meters per dollar\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517238 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517217 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517238 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517238 #edb/ref 1071 "A farmer estimates that she can sell $w(p)$ metric tons of crops at a price of $p$ euros per ton. What are the units of $\\frac{dw}{dp}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517238 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "edfb327e-a31c-5382-9b46-3e8f2986f4de" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517238 #edb/ref 1074 "We have that $w(p)$ is measured in tons and $p$ is measured in euros per ton. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w(p): & \\text{tons} \\\\ p: & \\text{euros per ton}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}w}{\\text{d}p}$ measures a change in $w(p)$ over a change in $p.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}w}{\\text{d}p}: & \\frac{\\text{tons}}{\\text{euros per ton}}=\\frac{\\text{squared tons}}{\\text{euros}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\text{d}w}{\\text{d}p}$ is measured in \"squared tons per euro\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517241 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186064271.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517241 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "e5d9c8a9-e502-52af-b588-07dab00deac0" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517244 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517245 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517244 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517244 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a circle. Let $A$ denote the area and let $d$ denote the diameter. The diameter can vary. What is the best interpretation of ${A}^{′}(d)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517244 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "111755c5-cf23-571f-80b3-7d2188af00e9" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517244 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $A(d)$ gives the area of the circle as a function of the diameter. So,\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(d)=\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}d}=\\frac{\\text{Instantaneous change in }A}{\\text{Change in }d}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${A}^{′}(d)$ is the rate at which the area changes as the diameter changes." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517252 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517278 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517252 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517252 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a circle. Let $r$ denote the radius and let $A$ denote the area. The area can vary. What is the best interpretation of ${r}^{′}(A)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517252 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2db9b7d7-ed71-53ad-8854-ff8c9958d80c" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517252 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $r(A)$ gives the radius of the circle as a function of the area. So,\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(A)=\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}A}=\\frac{\\text{Instantaneous change in }r}{\\text{Change in }A}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${r}^{′}(A)$ is the rate at which the radius changes as the area changes." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517269 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517259 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517269 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517269 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a triangle. Let $A$ denote the area and let $b$ denote the base. The height is fixed while the base can vary. What is the best interpretation of ${A}^{′}(b)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517269 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78888321-c4c8-5405-85ed-a9511cce407b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517269 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $A(b)$ gives the area of the triangle as a function of the base. So,\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(b)=\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}b}=\\frac{\\text{Instantaneous change in }A}{\\text{Change in }b}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${A}^{′}(b)$ is the rate at which the area changes as the base changes." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517270 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517287 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517270 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517270 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a circle. Let $d$ denote the diameter and let $A$ denote the area. The area can vary. What is the best interpretation of ${d}^{′}(A)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517270 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7dc965eb-d392-5939-9157-9118f513fabe" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517270 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $d(A)$ gives the diameter of the circle as a function of the area. So,\n\n$$\n{d}^{′}(A)=\\frac{\\text{d}d}{\\text{d}A}=\\frac{\\text{Instantaneous change in }d}{\\text{Change in }A}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${d}^{′}(A)$ is the rate at which the diameter changes as the area changes." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517275 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517253 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517275 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517275 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a circle. Let $C$ denote the circumference and let $d$ denote the diameter. The diameter can vary. What is the best interpretation of ${C}^{′}(d)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517275 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "80ab9cd8-11a3-5133-8d3d-b3fe10a410f1" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517275 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $C(d)$ gives the circumference of the circle as a function of the diameter. So,\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(d)=\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}d}=\\frac{\\text{Instantaneous change in }C}{\\text{Change in }d}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${C}^{′}(d)$ is the rate at which the circumference changes as the diameter changes." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517288 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517247 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517288 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517288 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a sphere. Let $S$ denote the surface area and let $r$ denote the radius. The radius can vary. What is the best interpretation of ${S}^{′}(r)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517288 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b6736675-f4da-5185-8d23-2b3b00c3b84e" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517288 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $S(r)$ gives the surface area of the sphere as a function of the radius. So,\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}r}=\\frac{\\text{Instantaneous change in }S}{\\text{Change in }r}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${S}^{′}(r)$ is the rate at which the surface area changes as the radius changes." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517297 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517256 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517297 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517297 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a sphere. Let $V$ denote the volume and let $d$ denote the diameter. The diameter can vary. What is the best interpretation of ${V}^{′}(d)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517297 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "caedbb1e-389e-57a9-af5a-ada3046983a3" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517297 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $V(d)$ gives the volume of the sphere as a function of the diameter. So,\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(d)=\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}d}=\\frac{\\text{Instantaneous change in }V}{\\text{Change in }d}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${V}^{′}(d)$ is the rate at which the volume changes as the diameter changes." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517299 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517289 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517299 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517299 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a rectangle. Let $A$ denote the area and let $h$ denote the height. The base is fixed while the height can vary. What is the best interpretation of ${A}^{′}(h)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517299 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d53b30d6-d985-5572-9fe3-a726e66964ae" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517299 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $A(h)$ gives the area of the rectangle as a function of the height. So,\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(h)=\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}h}=\\frac{\\text{Instantaneous change in }A}{\\text{Change in }h}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${A}^{′}(h)$ is the rate at which the area changes as the height changes." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517302 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517271 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517302 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517302 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a circle. Let $C$ denote the circumference and let $r$ denote the radius. The radius can vary. What is the best interpretation of ${C}^{′}(r)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517302 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ee562661-46bb-5be2-bdee-e4d17325d904" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517302 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $C(r)$ gives the circumference of the circle as a function of the radius. So,\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}r}=\\frac{\\text{Instantaneous change in }C}{\\text{Change in }r}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${C}^{′}(r)$ is the rate at which the circumference changes as the radius changes." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517305 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186064152.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517305 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "2e7d7756-d35c-521b-911d-12b0d2b502d2" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517311 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517307 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517311 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517311 #edb/ref 1071 "A model records the mass of grain in a silo at time $t$, measured in hours after observations begin.\n\n- $E(t)$ - the mass of grain in a silo, in kilograms.\n\n- $a(t)$ - the positive rate at which that amount is decreasing, in kilograms per hour.\n\nWhat is the relationship between $E(t)$ and $a(t)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517311 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a01eb6f-a624-502a-a2a4-9a6dfc01e40c" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517311 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $E(t)$ gives the mass of grain in a silo after $t$ hours. Then,\n\n$$\nE'(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}E(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }E}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, $E'(t)$ gives the instantaneous rate of change of that amount. The amount is decreasing, so the derivative is the negative of the positive loss rate $a(t)$. Therefore, the relationship is $E'(t)=-a(t)$.\n\nThe change ratio denotes an instantaneous rate: the limit of change ratios as the time interval tends to zero." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517312 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517314 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517312 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517312 #edb/ref 1071 "A model records the mass of grain in a silo at time $t$, measured in hours after observations begin.\n\n- $E(t)$ - the mass of grain in a silo, in kilograms.\n\n- $A(t)$ - the positive rate at which the amount is added, in kilograms per hour.\n\n- $L(t)$ - the signed contribution of removal to the rate of change, in kilograms per hour; $L(t)<0$ when removal occurs.\n\nWhat is the relationship between the three functions above?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517312 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "73bef49f-6ef2-549c-9aa9-8a94dc95239f" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517312 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $E(t)$ gives the mass of grain in a silo after $t$ hours. Then,\n\n$$\nE'(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}E(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }E}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, $E'(t)$ gives the instantaneous rate at which the amount changes at time $t$.\n\nIn addition, $L(t)$ already includes the negative sign of removal. We add the two signed contributions at time $t$. That is,\n\n$$\nE'(t)=A(t)+L(t).\n$$\n\nThe change ratio denotes an instantaneous rate: the limit of change ratios as the time interval tends to zero." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517320 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186064272.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517320 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "0f0d2bb8-5382-52a4-b4a4-3ac767edc02b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517331 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517370 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517331 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517331 #edb/ref 1071 "The rate $F$ at which a swimming pool is being filled can be modeled as $F(t) = 4t^{2} - 3t$ liters per minute, where $t$ is the time, in minutes, since the pool began to be filled. The rate at which water is leaking from the pool can be modeled as $L(t) = \\frac{1}{4}t^{2}$ liters per minute, where $t$ is also the time, in minutes, since the pool began to be filled.\n\nWhat is the rate of change of the volume of water in the pool $8$ minutes after the pool began to be filled?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517331 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4064584b-a92b-5c2b-aee4-39b559d5a1a6" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517331 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change of the volume of water is the filling rate minus the leaking rate.\n\n$$\nF(t)-L(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=8$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(8)-L(8) & =4(8)^{2}-3(8)-\\frac{1}{4}(8)^{2} \\\\  & =232-16 \\\\  & =216.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change is $216$ liters per minute." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517332 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517324 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517332 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517332 #edb/ref 1071 "The birth rate of crocodiles in a river is modeled by $B(t) = 4 \\cdot e^{0.25t}$ crocodiles per year, while the rate of death is modeled by $D(t) = 1 \\cdot e^{0.25t}$ crocodiles per year, where $t$ represents the number of years since the year $2000$.\n\nWhat was the rate of change of the crocodile population in $2004$? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517332 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "43cee7e4-6ff9-5eeb-9102-bb7cf335c204" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517332 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change of the crocodile population is the birth rate minus the death rate.\n\n$$\nB(t)-D(t).\n$$\n\nSince $2004$ is $4$ years after $2000$, substitute $t=4$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(4)-D(4) & =4\\cdot e^{0.25(4)}-1\\cdot e^{0.25(4)} \\\\  & =3\\cdot e^{1} \\\\  & \\approx 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change is $8$ crocodiles per year." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517340 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517353 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517340 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517340 #edb/ref 1071 "The supplied rate function is $h'(x)=\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{x}}$ thousand dollars per week, for $x>0$.\n\nWhat was the exact rate of change of the stock's value at week $144?$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ thousand dollars per week." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517340 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "631f3d25-1539-5b45-a5e3-359eb72f3729" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517340 #edb/ref 1074 "The function ${h}^{'}(x)$ models the rate of change of the value of the stock, applicable in the domain of $h(x).$\n\nThus, the rate of change of the value of the stock at $x=144$ weeks is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{'}(144) & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{144}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change in the stock price was $\\frac{1}{60}$ thousand dollars per week." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517344 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517339 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517344 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517344 #edb/ref 1071 "A factory begins work at $8\\text{:}00$ am. Its order rate is $O(t)=140t e^{-0.2t}$ cars per hour and its completion rate is $C(t)=90+15\\sin(2\\pi t/14)$ cars per hour, where $t$ is hours after work begins. What is the rate of change in the number of cars under construction at the 24-hour time $18\\text{:}30$? Orders enter construction immediately. Round your answer to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517344 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f4724c3-2921-55f4-a281-340d20927d04" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517344 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change is the order rate minus the completion rate. The elapsed time is $t=(1110-480)/60=10.5$ hours.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O(10.5)-C(10.5) & =140(10.5){e}^{-0.2(10.5)}-(90+15\\sin(\\frac{2\\pi }{14}(10.5))) \\\\  & =140(10.5){e}^{-2.1}-90-15\\sin(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}) \\\\  & =140(10.5){e}^{-2.1}-75 \\\\  & \\approx 105.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change is $105$ cars per hour." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517347 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517364 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517347 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517347 #edb/ref 1071 "A factory begins work at $8\\text{:}00$ am. Its order rate is $O(t)=190t e^{-0.2t}$ cars per hour and its completion rate is $C(t)=50+10\\sin(2\\pi t/10)$ cars per hour, where $t$ is hours after work begins. What is the rate of change in the number of cars under construction at the 24-hour time $10\\text{:}30$? Orders enter construction immediately. Round your answer to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517347 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8e5ec36a-ecef-5d7c-8e17-1882b5fa8983" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517347 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change is the order rate minus the completion rate. The elapsed time is $t=(630-480)/60=2.5$ hours.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O(2.5)-C(2.5) & =190(2.5){e}^{-0.2(2.5)}-(50+10\\sin(\\frac{2\\pi }{10}(2.5))) \\\\  & =190(2.5){e}^{-0.5}-50-10\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{2}) \\\\  & =190(2.5){e}^{-0.5}-60 \\\\  & \\approx 228.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change is $228$ cars per hour." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517352 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517362 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517352 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517352 #edb/ref 1071 "After charging the battery of a PC, the percentage of charge decreases at a rate of $c(t)=100\\cdot {5}^{-0.4t}$ percent per hour, where $t$ is the time, in hours, since the battery had completed its charge. What will be the rate of decrease after $10$ hours?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517352 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a5c334a0-b9d7-58f1-b30e-102327fa70dc" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517352 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we want to determine the rate of decrease in the amount of charge after $10$ hours, we have $t=10.$\n\nThus, the rate of decrease of the amount of charge after $10$ hours will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c(10) & =100\\cdot {5}^{-0.4(10)} \\\\  & =100\\cdot {5}^{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of decrease will be $\\frac{4}{25}$ percent per hour." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517360 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517350 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517360 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517360 #edb/ref 1071 "A factory begins work at $8\\text{:}00$ am. Its order rate is $O(t)=90t e^{-0.2t}$ cars per hour and its completion rate is $C(t)=20+15\\sin(2\\pi t/14)$ cars per hour, where $t$ is hours after work begins. What is the rate of change in the number of cars under construction at the 24-hour time $11\\text{:}30$? Orders enter construction immediately. Round your answer to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517360 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd6eb95d-7375-5343-986b-68f30ad5b2d2" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517360 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change is the order rate minus the completion rate. The elapsed time is $t=(690-480)/60=3.5$ hours.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O(3.5)-C(3.5) & =90(3.5){e}^{-0.2(3.5)}-(20+15\\sin(\\frac{2\\pi }{14}(3.5))) \\\\  & =90(3.5){e}^{-0.7}-20-15\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{2}) \\\\  & =90(3.5){e}^{-0.7}-35 \\\\  & \\approx 121.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change is $121$ cars per hour." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517369 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517366 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517369 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517369 #edb/ref 1071 "A factory begins work at $8\\text{:}00$ am. Its order rate is $O(t)=50t e^{-0.1t}$ cars per hour and its completion rate is $C(t)=80+20\\sin(2\\pi t/2)$ cars per hour, where $t$ is hours after work begins. What is the rate of change in the number of cars under construction at the 24-hour time $8\\text{:}30$? Orders enter construction immediately. Round your answer to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517369 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d1c8cfbb-47f4-5fb3-a9b0-9087d81918f8" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517369 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change is the order rate minus the completion rate. The elapsed time is $t=(510-480)/60=0.5$ hours.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O(0.5)-C(0.5) & =50(0.5){e}^{-0.1(0.5)}-(80+20\\sin(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}(0.5))) \\\\  & =50(0.5){e}^{-0.05}-80-20\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{2}) \\\\  & =50(0.5){e}^{-0.05}-100 \\\\  & \\approx -76.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change is $-76$ cars per hour." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517376 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517338 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517376 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517376 #edb/ref 1071 "A factory begins work at $8\\text{:}00$ am. Its order rate is $O(t)=100t e^{-0.1t}$ cars per hour and its completion rate is $C(t)=40+20\\sin(2\\pi t/6)$ cars per hour, where $t$ is hours after work begins. What is the rate of change in the number of cars under construction at the 24-hour time $9\\text{:}30$? Orders enter construction immediately. Round your answer to the nearest integer." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517376 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f2e135c6-4cc0-5a93-bc48-e0df9714cad2" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517376 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change is the order rate minus the completion rate. The elapsed time is $t=(570-480)/60=1.5$ hours.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O(1.5)-C(1.5) & =100(1.5){e}^{-0.1(1.5)}-(40+20\\sin(\\frac{2\\pi }{6}(1.5))) \\\\  & =100(1.5){e}^{-0.15}-40-20\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{2}) \\\\  & =100(1.5){e}^{-0.15}-60 \\\\  & \\approx 69.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change is $69$ cars per hour." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517380 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186064154.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517380 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "0588722c-3c3a-5cea-ab70-6b10fb7fbff3" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186550349 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186551363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550349 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186551418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550349 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186551433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550349 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550349 #edb/ref 1045 "Interpreting the Meaning of the Derivative in Context" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550349 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186551433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550349 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550349 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550349 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "57956ceb-a050-5fd1-9c8a-9b26480f8fc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550426 #edb/ref 1080 "{W}^{′}(t)=r(t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550426 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550426 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1b410e34-af3e-5a01-933c-fbe6349f45e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550451 #edb/ref 1080 "For the first $500$ items produced, the cost per item constantly increased at a rate of $0.01$ dollars per item" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550451 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592